I have enough for the gravity-charts side: `LineSeries` with `{type: 'line', name, data: [{x: timestamp, y: number}]}` and `xAxis: {type: 'datetime'}`.

Now for the widget design:

**Unified widget API**: A single `VizWidget` component with props like:

```tsx
type VizWidgetProps =
  | {mode: 'sales'; data: SalesData}
  | {mode: 'monitoring'; data: MonitoringData};
```

Or simpler and more in the spirit of "единый API виджета" — a single component `<VizWidget type="sales" | "monitoring" data={...} />` that internally maps to ChartKit types `gravity-charts` / `yagr`. The chart engines load lazily via chartkit plugins (React.lazy) — that's the key requirement, and ChartKit gives it out of the box.

I'll design:

```tsx
// src/widget/types.ts
export type SalesPoint = {date: number; revenue: number; ...}
export type MonitoringSeries = {name: string; color?; points...}

export type VizWidgetProps = {
  title?: string;
} & (
  | {mode: 'sales'; data: {...}}
  | {mode: 'monitoring'; data: {...}}
);
```

Actually, better: keep the widget API declarative — the caller passes a mode and plain row data; the widget builds the engine-specific config. That is a genuinely unified API: consumers never see `gravity-charts` vs `yagr`.

Widget structure:
- `src/widget/VizWidget.tsx` — main component: Card with header (title + mode switcher) and body rendering `<ChartKit type={...} data={...} />` inside a sized container.
- `src/widget/adapters.ts` — converts unified data → GravityChartsWidgetData / YagrWidgetData.
- `src/widget/chartkit-setup.ts` — registers plugins via `settings.set`.
- `src/App.tsx` — demo with SegmentedRadioGroup switcher + sample data.

Wait — the requirement says "переключателем между обычным линейным графиком продаж и плотным временным рядом мониторинга" — a switcher between the two modes. The switcher can be inside the widget (header) or outside in App. "Оба режима должны работать через единый API виджета" — both modes work through the widget's unified API. I'll put the mode switcher as part of the widget itself (header control), and the widget API accepts `mode` controlled prop + `onModeChange`, or uncontrolled default. Simpler: the widget exposes `mode` prop, and the demo App holds state and renders a switcher. But the task says "один виджет визуализации с переключателем" — one widget WITH a switcher. So the switcher should be part of the widget. I'll make the widget self-contained: header with title + SegmentedRadioGroup (uikit) to switch modes, and it swaps the data/config accordingly. And expose props so it can be controlled if desired? Keep it simple: uncontrolled inside widget with `defaultMode`, and a unified `data` prop containing both datasets? Hmm.

"Единый API виджета" — I think the cleanest interpretation: the widget accepts a discriminated union:

```tsx
<VizWidget
  title="..."
  mode="sales"           // controlled
  onModeChange={...}
  sales={{...}}
  monitoring={{...}}
/>
```

That's clunky. Better: the widget owns the switcher and accepts both datasets:

```tsx
<VizWidget
  title="Revenue & Infrastructure"
  defaultMode="sales"
  data={{
    sales: {points: [...]},
    monitoring: {series: [...]},
  }}
/>
```

Hmm, but a "unified API" more elegantly: single `data` prop whose shape is per-mode, plus a mode switcher. If the widget owns the switcher, it needs data for both modes available when switching. Unless switching the mode re-fetches. For the demo, having both datasets passed in is fine.

Alternative cleaner design: `VizWidget` takes `mode` and `data` (discriminated union), and the App renders the switcher. But task says widget WITH switcher.

I'll go with: `VizWidget` is self-contained with an internal mode switcher in its header, accepting:

```tsx
export type VizWidgetMode = 'sales' | 'monitoring';

export type VizWidgetData = {
  sales: SalesWidgetData;
  monitoring: MonitoringWidgetData;
};

export type VizWidgetProps = {
  title?: string;
  data: VizWidgetData;
  defaultMode?: VizWidgetMode;      // uncontrolled
  mode?: VizWidgetMode;             // controlled
  onModeChange?: (mode) => void;
  height?: number;
};
```

Internally:
- `settings.set({plugins: [GravityChartsPlugin, YagrPlugin]})` once at module level in the widget file (or a setup module imported by main). The docs say registry must be populated at app entry before render. Since the widget module does it at import time, that works.
- Adapters build `GravityChartsWidgetData` and `YagrWidgetData`.
- Renders `<ChartKit type={mode === 'sales' ? 'gravity-charts' : 'yagr'} data={...} />` in a fixed-height container.

Lazy loading: ChartKit plugin renderers are React.lazy — engines only fetched when that type renders. Plus I can additionally wrap... no, ChartKit's plugin laziness is the mechanism. Both plugins registered, but the yagr bundle only loads when monitoring mode is first shown and gravity-charts bundle when sales shown. That satisfies "соответствующие движки графиков должны загружаться лениво".

Also add `renderPluginLoader` with a uikit Loader/Skeleton for nice loading state.

uikit components: `Card`, `SegmentedRadioGroup`, `Text`, `Flex`. Need to check SegmentedRadioGroup API — it's uikit's segmented control. Let me check quickly in the installed uikit (7.43.0 — below 7.45 so no build/docs; check .d.ts).

The dataviz skill: colors. gravity-charts has its own default palette (documented default list above). For the sales chart, I can use 2 series (e.g., "2025" vs "2026" or "online"/"retail"). The charts default palette: ['#4DA2F1', '#FF3D64', ...]. Per dataviz skill I should validate palette... The validator checks a categorical palette. The charts package default palette is the design system's own; the skill says a design system supplies parameters (its ramps). Gravity UI charts default palette is the system palette. Using the design-system defaults is consistent with "the method consumes them unchanged" — the system supplies the palette. I could run the validator on the two/three colors I use, on both light and dark surfaces, to be diligent. The dataviz skill says "Run the validator before shipping any categorical palette". Let me validate the first 2-3 gravity-charts default colors on light and dark surfaces.

For yagr (monitoring), series colors need to be specified manually in graphs config (`color` per series). I'll use CSS variables? Yagr supports "CSS Variables in color names". I'll pick colors from the same palette for consistency. For dense monitoring, maybe 3 series: cpu, memory, network.

Also dataviz non-negotiables: legend for ≥2 series (gravity-charts legend enabled; yagr has built-in legend), hover tooltips (both engines have them by default), text in text tokens (uikit Text for titles), no dual axis (fine — single y-axis each).

Now let me check SegmentedRadioGroup in uikit 7.43.0. And Card API. Also check whether `@gravity-ui/charts` needs additional style imports? The charts package docs say uikit styles + ThemeProvider suffice. Yagr might need its own CSS? The chartkit yagr renderer has YagrWidget.css imported by itself. Fine.

Let me inspect uikit for SegmentedRadioGroup, Card, Loader, Text, Flex.

